1	// Fault-injection shims for the regression-test build of envelopefile.
2	//
3	// This file is linked together with src/main.cpp into the separate
4	// `envelopefile_testable` binary using the linker's --wrap option, so every
5	// open/write/fsync/close call made by the product code is routed through the
6	// __wrap_* functions below. The production binary is completely unaffected.
7	//
8	// Injection is controlled entirely through environment variables so the test
9	// driver can select a scenario per process run:
10	//
11	//   EF_TEST_TARGET          Only inject faults for this exact path. When
12	//                           unset, any file created with O_CREAT|O_EXCL is
13	//                           considered the key file under test.
14	//   EF_TEST_FAIL_WRITE=1    write() on the key file fails with EIO.
15	//   EF_TEST_PARTIAL_WRITE=N write() on the key file returns at most N bytes
16	//                           per call (the caller must loop to complete).
17	//   EF_TEST_FAIL_FSYNC=1    fsync() on the key file fails with EIO.
18	//   EF_TEST_FAIL_CLOSE=1    close() on the key file reports EIO (the
19	//                           descriptor is still really closed, matching the
20	//                           POSIX guarantee that close() releases the fd).
21	//
22	// For deterministic coverage of the write loop itself, a per-call script can
23	// be supplied:
24	//
25	//   EF_TEST_WRITE_SCRIPT=t1,t2,...
26	//                           Each token describes the outcome of one write()
27	//                           call on the key file, consumed in order; once the
28	//                           list is exhausted every further call succeeds
29	//                           normally. Tokens:
30	//                             ok    - write the whole requested buffer
31	//                             N     - short write: at most N (decimal) bytes
32	//                             intr  - fail with EINTR, no bytes written
33	//                             eio   - fail irrecoverably with EIO
34	//                             zero  - return 0 with bytes still pending
35	//
36	//   EF_TEST_WRITE_LOG=path  Record what the write loop actually did, so the
37	//                           test driver can prove byte continuity across
38	//                           retries. On the first key-file write the original
39	//                           32-byte key is snapshotted; every subsequent call
40	//                           is checked to advance from the current offset with
41	//                           a buffer that is a slice of that same snapshot
42	//                           (i.e. the key is neither regenerated, duplicated,
43	//                           nor dropped). The file receives one line:
44	//                             VERIFY <64 lowercase hex digits>
45	//                           followed by one line per write() call:
46	//                             CALL <offset> <requested> <returned> [<errno>]
47	//                           (the errno field is only present when returned is
48	//                           -1). A continuity violation is recorded as a
49	//                             VERIFY-FAILED <reason>
50	//                           line and aborts the process, since it means the
51	//                           product code violated its own write contract.
52	//
53	// Only the file descriptor opened for the key file is instrumented; all
54	// other I/O (stdout, stderr, OpenSSL internals, the log file itself) passes
55	// through untouched.
56	
